{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b3fbcb2735147531", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tMEASURING CROSS-CULTURAL STYLE DIFFUSION THROUGH ERA\n2\tCLASSIFICATION: US AND KOREAN POPULAR MUSIC\n3\tDasol Lee1\n4\tMinhee Lee1\n5\tSeonguk Ju1\n6\tDaewoong Kim1\n7\tHarin Lee2\n8\tDasaem Jeong1\n9\t1\n10\tMusic & Arts Learning (MALer) Lab, Sogang University, South Korea\n11\t2\n12\tUniversity of Cambridge\n13\tdasollee, dasaemj@sogang.ac.kr\n14\t\n15\tarXiv:2608.10980v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n16\t\n17\tABSTRACT\n18\tPopular music circulates globally while being locally reinterpreted, yet this process of cross-cultural style diffusion has rarely been quantified. We propose an eraclassification framework for measuring temporal alignment between chart cultures. CNN classifiers trained from\n19\tscratch on Billboard Hot 100 audio are applied to Korean\n20\tMelon chart songs. Korean chart songs from the 1960s\n21\tthrough the 1980s are consistently inferred as belonging\n22\tto earlier Billboard eras, by a median of about four to five\n23\tyears, while the same models remain unbiased on heldout Billboard audio. The offset then halves at the 1990s,\n24\tto roughly two to three years, and holds there through the\n25\t2000s. Reverse inference shows a complementary narrowing, and the pattern holds across architectures and seeds.\n26\tWe interpret these results as reflecting how globally circulating pop styles were locally adopted and progressively\n27\tsynchronized. The framework can be applied to other\n28\tpairs of chart cultures beyond the US–Korea case examined here.\n29\t1. INTRODUCTION\n30\tPopular music is a living mirror of its time, changing continuously as musical practices interact with technological\n31\tconditions, media infrastructures, and transnational cultural flows. Because the modern commercial pop industry\n32\tfirst established its large-scale paradigm in the US and UK\n33\tand later circulated globally [1, 5], many regional pop traditions developed through sustained encounters with these\n34\tinternationally dominant forms while retaining local musical identities.\n35\tSouth Korean popular music is a particularly instructive case. Its trajectory is extensively documented in musicological literature (Section 2), from the absorption of\n36\tWestern styles through US military performance circuits\n37\tin the 1960s to the globally dominant K-pop system of the\n38\t\n39\t© D. Lee, M. Lee, S. Ju, D. Kim, H. Lee, and D. Jeong. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n40\t(CC BY 4.0). Attribution: D. Lee, M. Lee, S. Ju, D. Kim, H. Lee, and D.\n41\tJeong, “Measuring Cross-Cultural Style Diffusion Through Era Classification: US and Korean Popular Music”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society\n42\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n43\t\n44\tFigure 1. Schematic overview: a CNN era classifier\n45\ttrained on Billboard audio is applied to Melon chart songs.\n46\tThe difference between inferred and actual chart-entry eras\n47\tquantifies cross-cultural temporal alignment.\n48\t\n49\t2000s. Despite these rich qualitative narratives, the acoustic dimensions of this history have been examined less frequently in quantitative terms.\n50\tThis paper examines that trajectory computationally.\n51\tWe ask whether audio characteristics that a model associates with particular Billboard eras can also be found in\n52\tKorean chart music, and if so, with what temporal relationship. We refer to this phenomenon as cross-cultural style\n53\tdiffusion, operationalized here as the temporal alignment\n54\tof era-sensitive audio patterns between two chart ecosystems. This framing presupposes no direct causal influence;\n55\tit measures the degree to which the two occupy similar positions on a learned temporal axis.\n56\tOur core idea is illustrated in Figure 1. We train an era\n57\tclassifier exclusively on Billboard Hot 100 audio, using\n58\tCNN architectures trained from scratch to avoid data leakage from pre-trained models whose corpora likely include\n59\tKorean music, and apply it to Melon chart songs. If the\n60\tmodel systematically infers an earlier Billboard era for a\n61\tMelon song than its actual chart-entry year, this discrepancy is measured as a negative era offset, suggesting that\n62\tthe song’s audio characteristics are more similar to those\n63\tof older Billboard music than to its contemporaries.\n64\tUsing hierarchical era classification, we find quantitative evidence consistent with long-discussed historical patterns. Korean chart songs first appearing before the 1990s\n65\tare consistently inferred as belonging to earlier Billboard\n66\t\n67\t\feras, by a median of about four to five years. This offset\n68\tthen contracts to roughly two to three years in the 1990s\n69\tand remains at that level through the 2000s.\n70\tThe contributions of this paper are as follows:\n71\t• We propose an era-based cross-cultural inference\n72\tframework that uses chronological time—a reference defined consistently for both cultures, and independently of culturally variable genre or mood\n73\ttaxonomies—for quantifying temporal alignment\n74\tbetween chart ecosystems.\n75\t• We construct large-scale Billboard (∼22k tracks)\n76\tand Melon (∼2.2k tracks) audio datasets with artistaware splits designed for cross-domain era inference.\n77\t• We provide empirical evidence that the crossdomain era offset between US and Korean chart music halves at the 1990s and holds thereafter, a pattern that replicates across architectures and seeds\n78\tand is validated against in-domain predictions from\n79\tthe same models.\n80\t2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n81\t2.1 Historical Context of Korean Popular Music\n82\tFollowing the Korean War, the US 8th Army bases served\n83\tas a major conduit through which Western popular music\n84\tentered South Korea [22, 23]. In the 1960s and 1970s, figures such as Shin Joong-hyun and the band Sanullim introduced folk and psychedelic rock [25, 26, 31], drawing on\n85\tWestern formats such as blues-derived structures and electric instrumentation [32,33] while infusing them with local\n86\taesthetics [22,24]. The debut of Seo Taiji and Boys in 1992\n87\tthen marked the large-scale entry of modern dance music\n88\tand rap into the Korean mainstream [28,30]; aided by rapid\n89\teconomic growth and changes in media technology [27],\n90\tthe industry moved toward the contemporary K-pop system of the 2000s [29]. Section 6 revisits this trajectory\n91\tagainst our measurements.\n92\t2.2 Computational Analysis of Musical Evolution\n93\tLong-term change in popular music has been examined\n94\tcomputationally using large audio corpora. Serrà et al. [6]\n95\trevealed statistical trends in pitch, timbre, and loudness\n96\tacross several decades of the Million Song Dataset [7];\n97\tMauch et al. [11] tracked the rise and fall of stylistic clusters in the Billboard Hot 100; and Interiano et al. [2] documented long-term shifts in audio attributes across over\n98\t500,000 UK releases. These studies demonstrate that erasensitive acoustic signatures can be captured at scale, but\n99\tthey primarily describe feature-level trends within a single\n100\tcultural context.\n101\tParallel to these feature-level analyses, predicting the\n102\texact release year or decade of a track has become a standard MIR task [7, 17]. While typically treated as an endtask for metadata generation, a complementary line of\n103\twork uses classifiers themselves as analytical lenses. For\n104\t\n105\tinstance, Nie [3] trained genre classifiers on different temporal cohorts of songs from a Chinese music platform and\n106\tshowed that fluctuations in accuracy can signal stylistic\n107\tevolution rather than algorithmic failure. Our framework\n108\tshares this insight of reading classification output as a cultural signal, but extends it from within-culture genre evolution to cross-cultural temporal alignment via era prediction.\n109\t2.3 Cross-Cultural MIR\n110\tCross-cultural MIR research has begun to compare musical corpora across countries and traditions. Liew et al. [12]\n111\tused chart co-occurrence networks from 30 countries to\n112\tquantify the cross-cultural reach of Anglo-American popular music, while Papaioannou et al. [13] examined crosscultural transfer learning across regional datasets, suggesting that learned audio representations carry transferable\n113\tstructural information. These studies operate on a spatial\n114\taxis by comparing countries at a given time, whereas our\n115\twork adds a temporal axis by asking when on one culture’s\n116\ttimeline another culture’s songs are positioned.\n117\tA key design choice in cross-cultural comparison is the\n118\tlabel space. However, cross-cultural label spaces are inherently problematic. Empirical studies show that genre\n119\ttaxonomies and mood ratings exhibit substantial disagreement across different cultural listener populations, and existing taxonomies often carry Western biases [8–10,14,15].\n120\tConsequently, chronological time, defined independently\n121\tof cultural context, provides a comparative axis that is less\n122\tdependent on culturally variable labels. We therefore use\n123\tera classification as a comparative lens: rather than asking\n124\twhether two cultures share genre or mood labels, we ask\n125\twhere songs from one culture fall on a timeline learned\n126\tfrom the other.\n127\t3. DATASETS\n128\t3.1 Billboard Dataset\n129\tWe used the Billboard Hot 100 charts (1958–March 2024),\n130\tretaining only the initial chart entry per song to prevent\n131\trepeated exposure from periodic re-entries (e.g., holiday\n132\tsongs), yielding 31,092 unique entries. To maintain an uncontaminated US pop domain for era-classifier training, we\n133\tremoved 68 tracks primarily credited to Korean acts (e.g.,\n134\tWonder Girls, BTS), as they exhibit distinct characteristics of contemporary K-pop. Secondary features by Korean\n135\tartists were retained.\n136\tFor audio collection, we queried YouTube and filtered\n137\tout non-original versions (e.g., live, cover, music videos)\n138\tusing keyword heuristics and fuzzy string matching on\n139\tmetadata. Through this pipeline, we obtained audio for\n140\t22,002 tracks.\n141\t3.2 Melon Dataset\n142\tFor the Korean dataset, we used the Melon chart, which is\n143\tuniquely suited to this study as it is the only major chart\n144\taggregating historical rankings since the 1960s. Its format\n145\t\n146\t\fDataset\n147\t\n148\tSplit\n149\t\n150\t1960s 1970s 1980s 1990s 2000s 2010s\n151\t\n152\tTrain\n153\tBillboard Val.\n154\tTest\n155\t\n156\t4769\n157\t1005\n158\t987\n159\t\n160\t2850\n161\t555\n162\t484\n163\t\n164\t1970\n165\t294\n166\t404\n167\t\n168\t1600\n169\t279\n170\t191\n171\t\n172\t1585\n173\t134\n174\t123\n175\t\n176\t4387\n177\t153\n178\t232\n179\t\n180\tTrain\n181\tVal.\n182\tTest\n183\t\n184\t34\n185\t18\n186\t14\n187\t\n188\t191\n189\t23\n190\t37\n191\t\n192\t410\n193\t48\n194\t44\n195\t\n196\t494\n197\t94\n198\t85\n199\t\n200\t533\n201\t121\n202\t125\n203\t\n204\t0\n205\t0\n206\t0\n207\t\n208\tMelon\n209\t\n210\tTable 1. Tracks per decade in each split. Songs from\n211\t1958–1959 are grouped with the 1960s, 2020–2024 with\n212\tthe 2010s.\n213\tevolved from annual lists (1964–1984) to weekly formats\n214\tthereafter. Using a pipeline analogous to that for Billboard,\n215\twe collected audio for approximately 2,200 tracks that entered the chart between 1964 and 2009.\n216\tWe intentionally capped the Melon timeline at 2009 to\n217\tobserve the historical period of unidirectional style adoption. Because the full-scale globalization of K-pop in the\n218\t2010s led to bidirectional cultural flows [4, 29], restricting\n219\tour timeline to pre-2010 keeps the measured era offset interpretable as local charts converging toward global trends\n220\trather than mixing with them.\n221\tFor both datasets, era labels are assigned based on the\n222\tdate of first chart entry rather than the release date. First\n223\tchart entry more directly reflects when a song entered collective listening and commercial circulation, which is particularly relevant for studying style diffusion across chart\n224\tecosystems. This choice means that re-entries, posthumous re-releases, and remakes receive labels reflecting\n225\ttheir chart circulation date rather than their original production date.\n226\t3.3 Artist-Aware Split and Balancing\n227\tTo prevent artist leakage (i.e., the model recognizing a\n228\tsinger’s voice rather than era-sensitive musical patterns),\n229\twe split both datasets by artist. Because collaborations\n230\tcreate implicit links between artists, we constructed a collaboration graph in which two artists are connected if they\n231\tshare at least one chart entry, and identified its connected\n232\tcomponents. Each connected component was assigned as\n233\ta whole to train, validation, or test with an approximate\n234\tratio of 8:1:1. Artists with no collaborations formed singleton components. The resulting split sizes are shown in\n235\tTable 1.\n236\tTo mitigate class imbalance across eras, we applied\n237\tclass-balanced undersampling at the decade level during\n238\ttraining. We note that the Melon chart’s shift from annual to weekly format means that earlier decades (1960s–\n239\t1970s) contain substantially fewer tracks than later ones;\n240\tthis should be kept in mind when interpreting per-decade\n241\tresults for those periods.\n242\t4. METHOD\n243\tWe formulate the measurement of cross-cultural temporal\n244\talignment as cross-domain era classification. A classifier\n245\ttrained to predict chart-entry eras in one culture is applied\n246\t\n247\tto songs from another; the systematic discrepancy between\n248\tpredicted and actual eras quantifies the temporal offset.\n249\tConcretely, let s be a song whose actual chart-entry era\n250\tis es . When a Billboard-trained model infers an era ê(s)\n251\tfor a Melon song, the era offset is\n252\t∆(s) = ê(s) − es .\n253\t\n254\t(1)\n255\t\n256\tA negative mean offset indicates that those songs are positioned earlier on the Billboard-derived temporal axis than\n257\ttheir actual chart-entry dates would suggest. At inference time, each track is segmented into non-overlapping\n258\t30-second windows, their year-level softmax outputs are\n259\taveraged, and ê(s) is the year holding the largest averaged probability. Decade-level summaries are obtained via\n260\tGaussian kernel density estimation (bandwidth = 1 year);\n261\twe report the mode of each distribution.\n262\t4.1 Hierarchical Era Prediction\n263\tTreating year-level prediction as a flat multi-class problem\n264\tignores the ordinal structure of time. We therefore train\n265\tthe model to jointly predict the era at four hierarchical levels: decade, half-decade, quarter-decade, and year. Since\n266\tthe Billboard dataset spans 1958–2024, songs from 1958–\n267\t1959 are assigned to the 1960s and songs from 2020–2024\n268\tto the 2010s. To maintain a consistent tree in which every\n269\tchild node is wholly contained within its parent, the third\n270\tlevel divides each half-decade into intervals of 2 or 3 years\n271\trather than exactly 2.5, ensuring that no year maps to two\n272\tdifferent third-level nodes.\n273\tWe prefer this to direct year regression because a\n274\tsquared-error objective would smooth away the songs of\n275\tmost interest here. Some tracks carry traits of two separated eras—12 % of Melon tracks receive a year distribution with two well-separated peaks—and a classifier keeps\n276\tboth, whereas regression would answer with the midpoint,\n277\ta year resembling neither.\n278\tEach hierarchical level has an independent softmax\n279\thead, and the cross-entropy loss is summed over levels:\n280\tP3\n281\t(ℓ)\n282\tLCE = ℓ=0 LCE , where ℓ indexes the four levels from\n283\tdecade to year. Because the heads predict independently,\n284\ttheir outputs may be mutually inconsistent, so we add a\n285\thierarchical consistency loss LHC [21] penalizing parent–\n286\tchild violations, giving Ltotal = LCE + λLHC with λ=1\n287\tthroughout.\n288\t4.2 Audio Representation and Training Protocol\n289\tAll audio was converted to mono at 16 kHz, deliberately\n290\tdiscarding the stereo image and everything above 8 kHz,\n291\twhere mastering differences are most audible. Spectrograms follow each architecture’s original settings. 1 During training, a random 30-second crop is drawn from each\n292\ttrack per epoch.\n293\tWe trained all models with the Adam optimizer (lr = 1×\n294\t−4\n295\t10 ) and a batch size of 64 for 30 000 iterations, validating every 500, and retained the checkpoint with the highest\n296\t1 Code, chart-entry labels with YouTube IDs, artist-aware splits, and\n297\tfull training configurations: https://github.com/malerlab/\n298\tbillboard-melon-era\n299\t\n300\t\fDecade\n301\t\n302\tCNN\n303\t\n304\tFCN\n305\t\n306\tSCNN\n307\t\n308\tSCNNR\n309\t\n310\tMusicnn\n311\t\n312\tCRNN\n313\t\n314\t60s\n315\t70s\n316\t80s\n317\t90s\n318\t00s\n319\t10s\n320\t\n321\t90.8\n322\t66.3\n323\t78.0\n324\t56.8\n325\t74.8\n326\t35.3\n327\t\n328\t84.3\n329\t84.6\n330\t68.8\n331\t64.6\n332\t85.4\n333\t30.2\n334\t\n335\t89.4\n336\t69.8\n337\t85.9\n338\t68.8\n339\t79.7\n340\t33.6\n341\t\n342\t83.7\n343\t80.1\n344\t73.3\n345\t60.4\n346\t72.4\n347\t38.8\n348\t\n349\t84.6\n350\t66.9\n351\t83.7\n352\t61.5\n353\t73.2\n354\t50.9\n355\t\n356\t81.1\n357\t70.6\n358\t88.4\n359\t57.8\n360\t87.0\n361\t28.9\n362\t\n363\tMacro\n364\tMicro\n365\t\n366\t67.0\n367\t75.0\n368\t\n369\t69.6\n370\t75.1\n371\t\n372\t71.2\n373\t77.5\n374\t\n375\t68.1\n376\t74.6\n377\t\n378\t70.1\n379\t75.3\n380\t\n381\t69.0\n382\t73.7\n383\t\n384\tTable 2. Track-level era classification accuracy (%) on the\n385\tBillboard test set at the decade level, for the seed-77 run\n386\tof each architecture. Across all 18 runs, macro accuracy is\n387\t69.0±2.0 and micro accuracy 75.3±1.5.\n388\tvalidation macro accuracy. To mitigate class imbalance,\n389\ttraining batches were constructed by undersampling each\n390\tdecade to match the size of the smallest class (2000s, 1 585\n391\ttracks). This procedure is re-randomized every epoch. Every architecture was trained under three random seeds, giving the 18 runs over which we report variability below.\n392\t4.3 Model Selection Strategy\n393\tA central design requirement is that the era classifier must\n394\tbe trained exclusively on audio from a single chart culture\n395\tso that its temporal representations are derived from that\n396\tculture alone. Large-scale pre-trained music foundation\n397\tmodels such as MERT or Jukebox are trained on internetscale corpora that very likely include Korean popular music; fine-tuning such models would risk cross-cultural data\n398\tleakage, confounding the measurement we aim to perform.\n399\tWe therefore use CNN architectures trained from\n400\tscratch. We evaluated five models commonly used in\n401\tMIR [16]—FCN [17], ShortChunkCNN and its residual\n402\tvariant (ShortChunkCNN_Res) [18], musicnn [19], and\n403\tCRNN [20]—plus a simple baseline of three 2D convolutional layers (kernel size 3), each followed by batch normalization, ReLU, and max pooling. Model sizes range\n404\tfrom approximately 86 k (baseline) to 837 k parameters.\n405\tThe goal is not to maximize era-prediction accuracy per\n406\tse, but to ensure that whatever temporal patterns the models learn are attributable solely to Billboard audio, making\n407\tcross-domain inference interpretable as a culturally uncontaminated lens.\n408\t5. RESULTS\n409\t5.1 In-Domain Performance on Billboard\n410\tAll six architectures achieved reasonable in-domain performance on the Billboard test set (Table 2), with decadelevel macro accuracy ranging from 67.0 % to 71.2 %, well\n411\tabove the 16.7 % chance level for six-class classification.\n412\tThe year-level confusion matrix (Figure 2) further shows\n413\tthat errors are not random: predictions cluster near the\n414\tdiagonal, indicating that the models capture a meaningful temporal gradient. This tendency weakens around the\n415\t2010s, which may reflect the rapid diversification of genres\n416\tand production techniques in recent popular music.\n417\t\n418\tFigure 2. Confusion matrix of year-level predictions on\n419\tthe Billboard test set, pooled over all runs. Even when\n420\tthe exact year is missed, predictions fall near the true era,\n421\tas the diagonal shows. The tendency weakens around the\n422\t2010s, possibly reflecting greater stylistic diversity in recent music.\n423\t1960s 1970s 1980s 1990s 2000s\n424\tMelon tracks (n)\n425\tMedian offset (yr)\n426\t\n427\t166\n428\t351\n429\t602\n430\t773\n431\t879\n432\t−4.7 −4.5 −4.1 −2.4 −2.7\n433\t±SD 0.6\n434\t0.7\n435\t0.8\n436\t0.8\n437\t1.4\n438\t\n439\tTable 3. Median era offset per decade for Melon songs\n440\tunder Billboard-trained models, mean ± SD across the 18\n441\truns. The same models are unbiased in domain (Figure 3).\n442\t5.2 Billboard-to-Melon Inference: Main Finding\n443\tWhen Billboard-trained models are applied to Melon\n444\tsongs, the prediction error is no longer centered around\n445\tzero (Figure 3), while on held-out Billboard audio the\n446\tsame models stay centered in every decade, with a median in-domain offset of at most 0.2 years. Melon songs\n447\tare consistently inferred as belonging to earlier Billboard\n448\teras.\n449\tTable 3 summarises the median era offset for each\n450\tdecade. The offset holds near −4.7, −4.5, and −4.1 years\n451\tacross the 1960s, 1970s, and 1980s. The 1960s value is\n452\tbounded from below because the model cannot predict eras\n453\tearlier than 1958, and the Melon corpus begins in 1964:\n454\tits earliest songs can be shifted at most six years into the\n455\tpast. In the 1990s the offset contracts to −2.4 years, a step\n456\treproduced in every run, and holds at that level through\n457\tthe 2000s (−2.7, the least stable value in the table and the\n458\tdecade where in-domain errors are most dispersed as well,\n459\tFigure 3). This pattern is consistent with musicological accounts suggesting that Korean popular music exhibited a\n460\ttemporal offset in adopting globally circulating styles in\n461\tearlier decades, and that this offset narrowed sharply after\n462\tthe 1990s transition without closing.\n463\t\n464\t\fFigure 4. KDE of year-level predictions for Melon songs\n465\t(Billboard → Melon), pooled over all runs. Every decade\n466\tpeaks left of zero. The 2000s curve is the only bimodal\n467\tone, with mass both at the origin and near −4 years: part\n468\tof the decade has synchronized and part has not. Inset: the\n469\tsame densities on the full prediction range.\n470\t1970s mode\n471\t2000s mode\n472\t\n473\t0.07\n474\t\n475\tFCN\n476\t\n477\t0.06\n478\t\n479\tCNN\n480\t\n481\tCRNN\n482\tSCNNR\n483\tSCNN Musicnn\n484\t\n485\t0.05\n486\t\n487\tDensity\n488\t\n489\tFigure 3. Prediction error distribution (quarter-decade\n490\tlevel) for Billboard (in-domain) and Melon (crossdomain), pooled over all runs, with the y-axis shared\n491\twithin each decade so the two domains are directly comparable. Billboard errors stay centered on zero in every\n492\tdecade while widening toward the 2000s; Melon errors are\n493\tdisplaced toward earlier eras throughout.\n494\t\n495\t0.04\n496\t0.03\n497\t0.02\n498\t0.01\n499\t\n500\tIn the 2000s the predictions also split in two (Figure 4):\n501\tsome songs are dated on-era, others still several years\n502\tearly, and no single number describes both. The split follows genre. Among the tracks dated on-era in at least\n503\t80 % of runs, the recurring names are dance and hip-hop\n504\tacts (BigBang, 2NE1, Epik High), while those dated consistently early are led by ballad and R&B singers (SG\n505\tWannabe, Brown Eyes, Wheesung).\n506\t5.3 Cross-Architecture Consistency\n507\tThe overall trend is not an artifact of a single architecture.\n508\tFigure 5 compares the 1970s and 2000s predictions across\n509\tall six models. Every model back-dates the 1970s, by between 3.7 and 6.5 years. Moving to the 2000s, four of\n510\tthe six shift four to six years toward zero: on most architectures the distance between the two chart cultures has\n511\tlargely closed over these three decades. The other two\n512\tdissent—the baseline CNN barely moves, and Musicnn\n513\tmoves slightly further back—so how much offset survives\n514\tinto the 2000s remains architecture-dependent.\n515\t5.4 Melon-to-Billboard Reverse Inference\n516\tBack-dating might simply be what a Billboard-trained\n517\tmodel does to unfamiliar audio; reversing the direction\n518\ttests that. Trained on Melon and applied to Billboard, the\n519\tmodels place Billboard songs later on the Melon-derived\n520\ttimeline, by amounts shrinking from about ten years in\n521\tthe 1960s to near zero in the 2000s (Figure 6)—the same\n522\t\n523\t0.00\n524\t\n525\t-15\n526\t\n527\t-10\n528\t\n529\t-5\n530\t\n531\t0\n532\t\n533\tPredicted - True(One Year)\n534\t\n535\t5\n536\t\n537\t10\n538\t\n539\t15\n540\t\n541\tFigure 5. Per-architecture shift of the era offset (Billboard\n542\t→ Melon), each model pooled over its seeds. Arrows run\n543\tfrom a model’s 1970s density mode (orange) to its 2000s\n544\tmode (blue).\n545\tnarrowing, recovered by models that never saw Billboard.\n546\tIt carries less weight: the Melon-trained model reaches\n547\t52.4 % macro accuracy on the artist-disjoint Melon test\n548\tsplit, against 76.3 % for the Billboard-trained models over\n549\tthe same five decades, and 9.5 % for the 1960s, whose\n550\ttraining partition holds 34 tracks. We read it as corroborating the direction of the effect rather than its magnitude.\n551\t6. CONTEXTUALIZING THE ERA OFFSET\n552\tTable 4 summarizes the model’s predictions for five representative Korean artists spanning four decades. We interpret these patterns in light of the structural conditions of\n553\tcross-cultural music flow.\n554\t6.1 Structural Delay under Limited Channels\n555\t(1960s–1970s)\n556\tUntil the late 1980s, Korean musicians accessed American popular music mainly through U.S. military base\n557\tperformances and AFKN radio [37, 39]. Choi Hee-jun,\n558\t\n559\t\fsuch as SM Entertainment and YG Entertainment (the latter founded by his former bandmate Yang Hyun-suk) to\n560\tproduce artists oriented toward global trends from inception. BigBang (YG) and Girls’ Generation (SM) are consistent with this reading, with median offsets near zero by\n561\t2007–09. We stress that a single watershed figure cannot\n562\tbe shown to have caused a corpus-level shift; what the\n563\tmeasurements show is that the structural gap contracted\n564\tsharply around this moment.\n565\t\n566\t7. DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS\n567\tFigure 6. KDE of year-level predictions for Billboard\n568\tsongs (Melon → Billboard), pooled over seeds of the\n569\tMelon-trained model. Billboard songs are predicted toward later eras on the Melon-derived timeline.\n570\tArtist\n571\t\n572\tPeriod\n573\t\n574\tn Median (yr)\n575\t\n576\tSpread\n577\t\n578\tChoi Hee-jun\n579\tSanullim\n580\tSeo Taiji\n581\tBigBang\n582\tGirls’ Gen.\n583\t\n584\t1964–69\n585\t1978–88\n586\t1992–03\n587\t2007–09\n588\t2007–09\n589\t\n590\t12\n591\t13\n592\t10\n593\t14\n594\t6\n595\t\n596\t−6.1±0.7\n597\t−5.2±2.0\n598\t+2.8±2.2\n599\t−0.1±0.8\n600\t−1.0±1.1\n601\t\n602\t−8 to −2\n603\t−13 to +1\n604\t−5 to +10\n605\t−5 to +1\n606\t−6 to +1\n607\t\n608\tTable 4. Year-level era offsets for five representative Korean artists. Median is the mean ± SD of the per-artist\n609\tmedian across runs; spread is the 10th–90th percentile of\n610\tthat artist’s per-song offsets, after excluding two Sanullim\n611\tentries whose audio turned out to be non-original recordings.\n612\twho has recalled formative listening to artists such as Nat\n613\tKing Cole via military FM [41], and Sanullim, whose\n614\tpsychedelic rock drew on styles available through the same\n615\tchannels [38], are back-dated by 6.1 and 5.2 years despite\n616\ta full decade separating their activity. That the gap persists\n617\tacross genres and generations is consistent with its being\n618\ta property of the distribution infrastructure rather than of\n619\tindividual artists.\n620\t6.2 The Seo Taiji Watershed and Its Aftermath\n621\tSeo Taiji and Boys (debut 1992) are widely credited with\n622\tdismantling the broadcast-network-controlled Korean music market and catalyzing the rise of the talent-agency system that defines contemporary K-pop [34–36]. Shim [34]\n623\tnotes that “each of their albums was in itself a musical experimentation,” spanning new-jack-swing, metal with traditional instruments, gangsta rap, and techno. Seo Taiji is\n624\tthe only artist in Table 4 the model places ahead of his\n625\tchart dates, by +2.8 years and in 15 of 18 runs, at a time\n626\twhen his decade as a whole is still back-dated by 2.4 years.\n627\tHis songs also spread over a 15-year band, the widest in the\n628\ttable, against six to seven years for the consistently placed\n629\tartists on either side of the transition.\n630\tThis is the transition our decade-level results locate\n631\tin the 1990s, visible in a single career: by breaking the\n632\tbroadcast monopoly, he helped open the way for agencies\n633\t\n634\tWe proposed an era-classification framework to quantify\n635\tcross-cultural temporal alignment. Our main finding—a\n636\tfour-to-five year past-biased era offset in Korean chart music from the 1960s through the 1980s, halving at the 1990s\n637\tand holding at two to three years through the 2000s—gives\n638\tquantitative form to musicological accounts of how globally circulating styles were locally adopted and progressively synchronized.\n639\tThis study has several limitations. Our models cannot\n640\tseparate compositional style from recording technology: a\n641\tmel-spectrogram CNN responds to tape noise, compression, and mastering as readily as to harmony or instrumentation, so part of the measured offset is likely production\n642\tlag rather than stylistic lag. Related factors point the same\n643\tway: 30-second crops give no access to song-level form,\n644\tand artist-aware splitting does not control for producers,\n645\tengineers, or songwriters. Individual songs are dated imprecisely: across the 30-second crops of one song, predicted years vary with a mean SD of 3.3 years on Billboard\n646\tand 6.0 on Melon, so while our decade medians average\n647\tthat noise out, no one song’s offset is a precise dating. The\n648\taudio is matched from YouTube by title and artist and is\n649\tnot always the original recording. Finally, chart data reflects circulation rather than production, and the corpora\n650\tcover different spans.\n651\tTwo of these we can bound. Mastering style alone does\n652\tnot drive the era estimate: across 243 pairs of original and\n653\tremastered audio for the same songs, the predictions that\n654\tchange divide almost evenly between earlier and later, as\n655\tthe mono 16 kHz input intends. And restricting the corpus\n656\tto its best-matched 80 % moves no decade median by more\n657\tthan half a year.\n658\tWe read the era offset not as simple imitation, but as a\n659\ttrace of the evolving structural conditions of cross-cultural\n660\tmusic flows. Future work will extend the framework to\n661\tother chart systems, identify the acoustic cues behind the\n662\toffset, and apply era inference within a single corpus to\n663\tsurface artists predicted into later eras than their own [40].\n664\tOne such case already sits in our Billboard test set: the\n665\tmodels date The Beatles’ 1960s entries about two years\n666\tlater than their chart dates, against essentially zero for the\n667\tother 1960s songs in the split, a gap that holds in 15 of\n668\tthe 18 runs. The Beatles are widely credited with pioneering studio production, and the models place their music\n669\taccordingly.\n670\t\n671\t\f8. ACKNOWLEDGMENT\n672\tThis work was supported by the Ministry of Education of\n673\tthe Republic of Korea and the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF-2024S1A5C3A03046168).\n674\t9. REFERENCES\n675\t[1] M. Regev, Pop-rock music: Aesthetic cosmopolitanism\n676\tin late modernity. Cambridge, UK: Polity, 2013.\n677\t[2] M. Interiano, K. Kazemi, L. Wang, J. Yang, Z. Yu,\n678\tand N. Komarova, “Musical trends and predictability\n679\tof success in contemporary songs in and out of the\n680\ttop charts,” Royal Society Open Science, vol. 5, no. 5,\n681\tp. 171274, 2018.\n682\t[3] K. Nie, “Inaccurate prediction or genre evolution? Rethinking genre classification,” in Proc. of the 23rd Int.\n683\tSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Bengaluru, India, 2022, pp. 329–336.\n684\t[4] S. Miroudot, “What’s behind the ‘K’? Common audio\n685\tfeatures of Korean popular music before and after the\n686\trise of K-POP,” Popular Music, vol. 43, no. 3, pp. 1–28,\n687\t2024.\n688\t[5] M. Verboord and A. Brandellero, “The globalization of\n689\tpopular music, 1960–2010: A multi-level analysis of\n690\tmusic flows,” Communication Research, vol. 43, no. 3,\n691\tpp. 316–344, 2016.\n692\t[6] J. Serrà, A. Corral, M. Boguñá, M. Haro, and J. L. Arcos, “Measuring the evolution of contemporary western popular music,” Scientific Reports, vol. 2, pp. 1–6,\n693\t2012.\n694\t[7] T. Bertin-Mahieux, D. P. W. Ellis, B. Whitman, and\n695\tP. Lamere, “The million song dataset,” in Proc. of the\n696\t12th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf.,\n697\tMiami, Florida, USA, 2011.\n698\t[8] J. H. Lee, K. Choi, and X. Hu, “K-pop genres: A crosscultural exploration,” in Proc. of the 18th Int. Society\n699\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Suzhou, China,\n700\t2017, pp. 714–720.\n701\t[9] J. H. Lee and X. Hu, “Cross-cultural similarities and\n702\tdifferences in music mood perception,” in Proc. of the\n703\tiConference, 2014, pp. 259–269.\n704\t[10] E. Celen, P. van Rijn, H. Lee, and N. Jacoby, “GlobalMood: A cross-cultural benchmark for music emotion\n705\trecognition,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.08744, 2025.\n706\t[11] M. Mauch, R. MacCallum, M. Levy, and A. Leroi,\n707\t“The evolution of popular music: USA 1960–2010,”\n708\tRoyal Society Open Science, vol. 2, no. 5, 2015.\n709\t[12] K. Liew, V. Mishra, Y. Zhou, E. V. Epure, R. Hennequin, S. Wakamiya, and E. Aramaki, “Network analyses for cross-cultural music popularity,” in Proc. of\n710\tthe 23rd Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\n711\tConf., Bengaluru, India, 2022, pp. 298–304.\n712\t\n713\t[13] C. Papaioannou, E. Benetos, and A. Potamianos,\n714\t“From west to east: Who can understand the music of\n715\tthe others better?,” in Proc. of the 24th Int. Society for\n716\tMusic Information Retrieval Conf., Milan, Italy, 2023.\n717\t[14] X. Hu and J. H. Lee, “A cross-cultural study of music\n718\tmood perception between American and Chinese listeners,” in Proc. of the 13th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Porto, Portugal, 2012, pp. 535–\n719\t540.\n720\t[15] H. Lee, F. Hoeger, M. Schoenwiesner, M. Park, and\n721\tN. Jacoby, “Cross-cultural mood perception in pop\n722\tsongs and its alignment with mood detection algorithms,” in Proc. of the 22nd Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Online, 2021, pp. 1–8.\n723\t[16] M. Won, A. Ferraro, D. Bogdanov, and X. Serra, “Evaluation of CNN-based automatic music tagging models,” in Proc. of 17th Sound and Music Computing,\n724\t2020.\n725\t[17] K. Choi, G. Fazekas, and M. Sandler, “Automatic tagging using deep convolutional neural networks,” in\n726\tProc. of the 17th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., New York, NY, USA, 2016.\n727\t[18] M. Won, S. Chun, O. Nieto, and X. Serra, “Datadriven harmonic filters for audio representation learning,” in ICASSP 2020–2020 IEEE Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing, 2020, pp. 536–540.\n728\t[19] J. Pons, O. Nieto, M. Prockup, E. Schmidt, A. Ehmann,\n729\tand X. Serra, “End-to-end learning for music audio\n730\ttagging at scale,” in Proc. of the 19th Int. Society\n731\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Paris, France,\n732\t2018.\n733\t[20] K. Choi, G. Fazekas, M. Sandler, and K. Cho, “Convolutional recurrent neural networks for music classification,” in Proc. of 2017 IEEE Int. Conf. on Acoustics,\n734\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2017, pp. 1–\n735\t5.\n736\t[21] J. Wehrmann, R. Cerri, and R. Barros, “Hierarchical multi-label classification networks,” in Proc. of the\n737\t35th Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, Stockholm, Sweden, 2018.\n738\t[22] M. J. Jang, “How American popular music influenced\n739\tand propelled Korea’s popular music: Based on popular cultural phenomena,” Ph.D. dissertation, Dept. of\n740\tMusic, Daegu Catholic Univ., 2022.\n741\t[23] J.-H. Kim, “A study on the Korean people’s acceptance\n742\tand enjoyment of American modern popular music,”\n743\tThe Korean Folklore, vol. 56, pp. 67–96, 2012.\n744\t[24] E. Zhang, “The impact of Western popular music in\n745\tKorea: The development of ‘jazz song’ in the first\n746\thalf of the twentieth century,” Korea Observer, vol. 47,\n747\tno. 3, pp. 651–673, 2016.\n748\t\n749\t\f[25] C.-H. Ham and T.-S. Cho, “A study on the 1970’s\n750\tKorean psychedelic rock (focusing on Shin, Joonghyun),” J. of the Korea Academia-Industrial Cooperation Society, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 694–699, 2016.\n751\t\n752\t[38] P. H. Kim and H. Shin, “The birth of rok: Cultural imperialism, nationalism, and glocalization of rock music\n753\tin South Korea, 1964–1975,” positions: east asia cultures critique, vol. 18, no. 1, pp. 199–230, 2010.\n754\t\n755\t[26] M. J. Jang, “Music study of rock band Sanulrim: Focusing on contemporary Korean songs,” Master’s thesis, Daegu Catholic Univ., 2019.\n756\t\n757\t[39] P. H. Kim, “Korean rock’s journey from group sound to\n758\tindie punk,” in Made in Korea: Studies in Popular Music, H. Shin and S.-A. Lee, Eds. London: Routledge,\n759\t2016, pp. 143–162.\n760\t\n761\t[27] C. Lee, “A study on the change and acceptance of popular music from a social perspective: Focused on the\n762\tpop music market since the 20th century,” Master’s thesis, Baekseok Univ., Graduate School of Culture and\n763\tArts, 2020.\n764\t\n765\t[40] N. Collins, “Recording artist career comparison\n766\tthrough audio content analysis,” Royal Society Open\n767\tScience, vol. 12, 2025.\n768\t\n769\t[28] J. Jang, “A study about the sociocultural meaning of\n770\tSeo Tai Ji syndrome,” Master’s thesis, Dong-A Univ.,\n771\t2014.\n772\t[29] S.-W. Lee, “K-Pop: A peculiar encounter of Korean\n773\tand pop music – Inquiry into K-Pop’s Korean authenticity,” J. of Humanities, Seoul National Univ., vol. 73,\n774\tno. 1, pp. 77–103, 2016.\n775\t[30] K. Seong, “Four questions and answers about Seo\n776\tTaiji: Self-reflections,” Sahoebipyung (Social Criticism), vol. 26, pp. 200–211, 2000.\n777\t[31] Prog Rock Journal, “Korean psych rock legends Sanullim reissues of first three albums and a 2LP compilation\n778\twith later works,” Prog Rock Journal, 2023.\n779\t[32] K. M. Moist, “Introduction – Global psychedelia and\n780\tcounterculture,” Rock Music Studies, vol. 5, no. 3,\n781\tpp. 197–204, 2018.\n782\t[33] C. Morrison, “Psychedelic music in San Francisco:\n783\tStyle, context, and evolution,” Ph.D. dissertation, Concordia Univ., 2000.\n784\t[34] D. Shim, “Hybridity and the rise of Korean popular\n785\tculture in Asia,” Media, Culture & Society, vol. 28,\n786\tno. 1, pp. 25–44, 2006.\n787\t[35] E.-Y. Jung, “Articulating Korean youth culture through\n788\tglobal popular music styles: Seo Taiji’s use of rap\n789\tand metal,” in Korean Pop Music: Riding the Wave,\n790\tK. Howard, Ed. Folkestone, UK: Global Oriental,\n791\t2006, pp. 109–122.\n792\t[36] R. Maliangkay, “The popularity of individualism: The\n793\tSeo Taiji phenomenon in the 1990s,” in The Korean\n794\tPopular Culture Reader, K. H. Kim and Y. Choe, Eds.\n795\tDurham, NC: Duke University Press, 2013, pp. 296–\n796\t313.\n797\t[37] R. Maliangkay, “Supporting our boys: American military entertainment and Korean pop music in the 1950s\n798\tand early 1960s,” in Korean Pop Music: Riding the\n799\tWave, K. Howard, Ed. Folkestone, UK: Global Oriental, 2006, pp. 21–33.\n800\t\n801\t[41] H.-J. Choi, “Singer Choi Hee-jun: From law\n802\tschool to the stage” (in Korean), Catholic\n803\tTimes, Nov. 5, 2006. [Online]. Available:\n804\thttps://www.catholictimes.org/\n805\tarticle/200611120153178\n806\t\n807\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8b8e0a90-15e9-4873-9ccc-7dd1bd54dfa9", "uuid": "1dc40758-b35c-4bdb-9cc7-13857d6d31a2", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T07:34:52.059Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_10980.txt", "content": "MEASURING CROSS-CULTURAL STYLE DIFFUSION THROUGH ERA\nCLASSIFICATION: US AND KOREAN POPULAR MUSIC\nDasol Lee1\nMinhee Lee1\nSeonguk Ju1\nDaewoong Kim1\nHarin Lee2\nDasaem Jeong1\n1\nMusic & Arts Learning (MALer) Lab, Sogang University, South Korea\n2\nUniversity of Cambridge\ndasollee, dasaemj@sogang.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2608.10980v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nPopular music circulates globally while being locally reinterpreted, yet this process of cross-cultural style diffusion has rarely been quantified. We propose an eraclassification framework for measuring temporal alignment between chart cultures. CNN classifiers trained from\nscratch on Billboard Hot 100 audio are applied to Korean\nMelon chart songs. Korean chart songs from the 1960s\nthrough the 1980s are consistently inferred as belonging\nto earlier Billboard eras, by a median of about four to five\nyears, while the same models remain unbiased on heldout Billboard audio. The offset then halves at the 1990s,\nto roughly two to three years, and holds there through the\n2000s. Reverse inference shows a complementary narrowing, and the pattern holds across architectures and seeds.\nWe interpret these results as reflecting how globally circulating pop styles were locally adopted and progressively\nsynchronized. The framework can be applied to other\npairs of chart cultures beyond the US–Korea case examined here.\n1. INTRODUCTION\nPopular music is a living mirror of its time, changing continuously as musical practices interact with technological\nconditions, media infrastructures, and transnational cultural flows. Because the modern commercial pop industry\nfirst established its large-scale paradigm in the US and UK\nand later circulated globally [1, 5], many regional pop traditions developed through sustained encounters with these\ninternationally dominant forms while retaining local musical identities.\nSouth Korean popular music is a particularly instructive case. Its trajectory is extensively documented in musicological literature (Section 2), from the absorption of\nWestern styles through US military performance circuits\nin the 1960s to the globally dominant K-pop system of the\n\n© D. Lee, M. Lee, S. Ju, D. Kim, H. Lee, and D. Jeong. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n(CC BY 4.0). Attribution: D. Lee, M. Lee, S. Ju, D. Kim, H. Lee, and D.\nJeong, “Measuring Cross-Cultural Style Diffusion Through Era Classification: US and Korean Popular Music”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n\nFigure 1. Schematic overview: a CNN era classifier\ntrained on Billboard audio is applied to Melon chart songs.\nThe difference between inferred and actual chart-entry eras\nquantifies cross-cultural temporal alignment.\n\n2000s. Despite these rich qualitative narratives, the acoustic dimensions of this history have been examined less frequently in quantitative terms.\nThis paper examines that trajectory computationally.\nWe ask whether audio characteristics that a model associates with particular Billboard eras can also be found in\nKorean chart music, and if so, with what temporal relationship. We refer to this phenomenon as cross-cultural style\ndiffusion, operationalized here as the temporal alignment\nof era-sensitive audio patterns between two chart ecosystems. This framing presupposes no direct causal influence;\nit measures the degree to which the two occupy similar positions on a learned temporal axis.\nOur core idea is illustrated in Figure 1. We train an era\nclassifier exclusively on Billboard Hot 100 audio, using\nCNN architectures trained from scratch to avoid data leakage from pre-trained models whose corpora likely include\nKorean music, and apply it to Melon chart songs. If the\nmodel systematically infers an earlier Billboard era for a\nMelon song than its actual chart-entry year, this discrepancy is measured as a negative era offset, suggesting that\nthe song’s audio characteristics are more similar to those\nof older Billboard music than to its contemporaries.\nUsing hierarchical era classification, we find quantitative evidence consistent with long-discussed historical patterns. Korean chart songs first appearing before the 1990s\nare consistently inferred as belonging to earlier Billboard\n\n\feras, by a median of about four to five years. This offset\nthen contracts to roughly two to three years in the 1990s\nand remains at that level through the 2000s.\nThe contributions of this paper are as follows:\n• We propose an era-based cross-cultural inference\nframework that uses chronological time—a reference defined consistently for both cultures, and independently of culturally variable genre or mood\ntaxonomies—for quantifying temporal alignment\nbetween chart ecosystems.\n• We construct large-scale Billboard (∼22k tracks)\nand Melon (∼2.2k tracks) audio datasets with artistaware splits designed for cross-domain era inference.\n• We provide empirical evidence that the crossdomain era offset between US and Korean chart music halves at the 1990s and holds thereafter, a pattern that replicates across architectures and seeds\nand is validated against in-domain predictions from\nthe same models.\n2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n2.1 Historical Context of Korean Popular Music\nFollowing the Korean War, the US 8th Army bases served\nas a major conduit through which Western popular music\nentered South Korea [22, 23]. In the 1960s and 1970s, figures such as Shin Joong-hyun and the band Sanullim introduced folk and psychedelic rock [25, 26, 31], drawing on\nWestern formats such as blues-derived structures and electric instrumentation [32,33] while infusing them with local\naesthetics [22,24]. The debut of Seo Taiji and Boys in 1992\nthen marked the large-scale entry of modern dance music\nand rap into the Korean mainstream [28,30]; aided by rapid\neconomic growth and changes in media technology [27],\nthe industry moved toward the contemporary K-pop system of the 2000s [29]. Section 6 revisits this trajectory\nagainst our measurements.\n2.2 Computational Analysis of Musical Evolution\nLong-term change in popular music has been examined\ncomputationally using large audio corpora. Serrà et al. [6]\nrevealed statistical trends in pitch, timbre, and loudness\nacross several decades of the Million Song Dataset [7];\nMauch et al. [11] tracked the rise and fall of stylistic clusters in the Billboard Hot 100; and Interiano et al. [2] documented long-term shifts in audio attributes across over\n500,000 UK releases. These studies demonstrate that erasensitive acoustic signatures can be captured at scale, but\nthey primarily describe feature-level trends within a single\ncultural context.\nParallel to these feature-level analyses, predicting the\nexact release year or decade of a track has become a standard MIR task [7, 17]. While typically treated as an endtask for metadata generation, a complementary line of\nwork uses classifiers themselves as analytical lenses. For\n\ninstance, Nie [3] trained genre classifiers on different temporal cohorts of songs from a Chinese music platform and\nshowed that fluctuations in accuracy can signal stylistic\nevolution rather than algorithmic failure. Our framework\nshares this insight of reading classification output as a cultural signal, but extends it from within-culture genre evolution to cross-cultural temporal alignment via era prediction.\n2.3 Cross-Cultural MIR\nCross-cultural MIR research has begun to compare musical corpora across countries and traditions. Liew et al. [12]\nused chart co-occurrence networks from 30 countries to\nquantify the cross-cultural reach of Anglo-American popular music, while Papaioannou et al. [13] examined crosscultural transfer learning across regional datasets, suggesting that learned audio representations carry transferable\nstructural information. These studies operate on a spatial\naxis by comparing countries at a given time, whereas our\nwork adds a temporal axis by asking when on one culture’s\ntimeline another culture’s songs are positioned.\nA key design choice in cross-cultural comparison is the\nlabel space. However, cross-cultural label spaces are inherently problematic. Empirical studies show that genre\ntaxonomies and mood ratings exhibit substantial disagreement across different cultural listener populations, and existing taxonomies often carry Western biases [8–10,14,15].\nConsequently, chronological time, defined independently\nof cultural context, provides a comparative axis that is less\ndependent on culturally variable labels. We therefore use\nera classification as a comparative lens: rather than asking\nwhether two cultures share genre or mood labels, we ask\nwhere songs from one culture fall on a timeline learned\nfrom the other.\n3. DATASETS\n3.1 Billboard Dataset\nWe used the Billboard Hot 100 charts (1958–March 2024),\nretaining only the initial chart entry per song to prevent\nrepeated exposure from periodic re-entries (e.g., holiday\nsongs), yielding 31,092 unique entries. To maintain an uncontaminated US pop domain for era-classifier training, we\nremoved 68 tracks primarily credited to Korean acts (e.g.,\nWonder Girls, BTS), as they exhibit distinct characteristics of contemporary K-pop. Secondary features by Korean\nartists were retained.\nFor audio collection, we queried YouTube and filtered\nout non-original versions (e.g., live, cover, music videos)\nusing keyword heuristics and fuzzy string matching on\nmetadata. Through this pipeline, we obtained audio for\n22,002 tracks.\n3.2 Melon Dataset\nFor the Korean dataset, we used the Melon chart, which is\nuniquely suited to this study as it is the only major chart\naggregating historical rankings since the 1960s. Its format\n\n\fDataset\n\nSplit\n\n1960s 1970s 1980s 1990s 2000s 2010s\n\nTrain\nBillboard Val.\nTest\n\n4769\n1005\n987\n\n2850\n555\n484\n\n1970\n294\n404\n\n1600\n279\n191\n\n1585\n134\n123\n\n4387\n153\n232\n\nTrain\nVal.\nTest\n\n34\n18\n14\n\n191\n23\n37\n\n410\n48\n44\n\n494\n94\n85\n\n533\n121\n125\n\n0\n0\n0\n\nMelon\n\nTable 1. Tracks per decade in each split. Songs from\n1958–1959 are grouped with the 1960s, 2020–2024 with\nthe 2010s.\nevolved from annual lists (1964–1984) to weekly formats\nthereafter. Using a pipeline analogous to that for Billboard,\nwe collected audio for approximately 2,200 tracks that entered the chart between 1964 and 2009.\nWe intentionally capped the Melon timeline at 2009 to\nobserve the historical period of unidirectional style adoption. Because the full-scale globalization of K-pop in the\n2010s led to bidirectional cultural flows [4, 29], restricting\nour timeline to pre-2010 keeps the measured era offset interpretable as local charts converging toward global trends\nrather than mixing with them.\nFor both datasets, era labels are assigned based on the\ndate of first chart entry rather than the release date. First\nchart entry more directly reflects when a song entered collective listening and commercial circulation, which is particularly relevant for studying style diffusion across chart\necosystems. This choice means that re-entries, posthumous re-releases, and remakes receive labels reflecting\ntheir chart circulation date rather than their original production date.\n3.3 Artist-Aware Split and Balancing\nTo prevent artist leakage (i.e., the model recognizing a\nsinger’s voice rather than era-sensitive musical patterns),\nwe split both datasets by artist. Because collaborations\ncreate implicit links between artists, we constructed a collaboration graph in which two artists are connected if they\nshare at least one chart entry, and identified its connected\ncomponents. Each connected component was assigned as\na whole to train, validation, or test with an approximate\nratio of 8:1:1. Artists with no collaborations formed singleton components. The resulting split sizes are shown in\nTable 1.\nTo mitigate class imbalance across eras, we applied\nclass-balanced undersampling at the decade level during\ntraining. We note that the Melon chart’s shift from annual to weekly format means that earlier decades (1960s–\n1970s) contain substantially fewer tracks than later ones;\nthis should be kept in mind when interpreting per-decade\nresults for those periods.\n4. METHOD\nWe formulate the measurement of cross-cultural temporal\nalignment as cross-domain era classification. A classifier\ntrained to predict chart-entry eras in one culture is applied\n\nto songs from another; the systematic discrepancy between\npredicted and actual eras quantifies the temporal offset.\nConcretely, let s be a song whose actual chart-entry era\nis es . When a Billboard-trained model infers an era ê(s)\nfor a Melon song, the era offset is\n∆(s) = ê(s) − es .\n\n(1)\n\nA negative mean offset indicates that those songs are positioned earlier on the Billboard-derived temporal axis than\ntheir actual chart-entry dates would suggest. At inference time, each track is segmented into non-overlapping\n30-second windows, their year-level softmax outputs are\naveraged, and ê(s) is the year holding the largest averaged probability. Decade-level summaries are obtained via\nGaussian kernel density estimation (bandwidth = 1 year);\nwe report the mode of each distribution.\n4.1 Hierarchical Era Prediction\nTreating year-level prediction as a flat multi-class problem\nignores the ordinal structure of time. We therefore train\nthe model to jointly predict the era at four hierarchical levels: decade, half-decade, quarter-decade, and year. Since\nthe Billboard dataset spans 1958–2024, songs from 1958–\n1959 are assigned to the 1960s and songs from 2020–2024\nto the 2010s. To maintain a consistent tree in which every\nchild node is wholly contained within its parent, the third\nlevel divides each half-decade into intervals of 2 or 3 years\nrather than exactly 2.5, ensuring that no year maps to two\ndifferent third-level nodes.\nWe prefer this to direct year regression because a\nsquared-error objective would smooth away the songs of\nmost interest here. Some tracks carry traits of two separated eras—12 % of Melon tracks receive a year distribution with two well-separated peaks—and a classifier keeps\nboth, whereas regression would answer with the midpoint,\na year resembling neither.\nEach hierarchical level has an independent softmax\nhead, and the cross-entropy loss is summed over levels:\nP3\n(ℓ)\nLCE = ℓ=0 LCE , where ℓ indexes the four levels from\ndecade to year. Because the heads predict independently,\ntheir outputs may be mutually inconsistent, so we add a\nhierarchical consistency loss LHC [21] penalizing parent–\nchild violations, giving Ltotal = LCE + λLHC with λ=1\nthroughout.\n4.2 Audio Representation and Training Protocol\nAll audio was converted to mono at 16 kHz, deliberately\ndiscarding the stereo image and everything above 8 kHz,\nwhere mastering differences are most audible. Spectrograms follow each architecture’s original settings. 1 During training, a random 30-second crop is drawn from each\ntrack per epoch.\nWe trained all models with the Adam optimizer (lr = 1×\n−4\n10 ) and a batch size of 64 for 30 000 iterations, validating every 500, and retained the checkpoint with the highest\n1 Code, chart-entry labels with YouTube IDs, artist-aware splits, and\nfull training configurations: https://github.com/malerlab/\nbillboard-melon-era\n\n\fDecade\n\nCNN\n\nFCN\n\nSCNN\n\nSCNNR\n\nMusicnn\n\nCRNN\n\n60s\n70s\n80s\n90s\n00s\n10s\n\n90.8\n66.3\n78.0\n56.8\n74.8\n35.3\n\n84.3\n84.6\n68.8\n64.6\n85.4\n30.2\n\n89.4\n69.8\n85.9\n68.8\n79.7\n33.6\n\n83.7\n80.1\n73.3\n60.4\n72.4\n38.8\n\n84.6\n66.9\n83.7\n61.5\n73.2\n50.9\n\n81.1\n70.6\n88.4\n57.8\n87.0\n28.9\n\nMacro\nMicro\n\n67.0\n75.0\n\n69.6\n75.1\n\n71.2\n77.5\n\n68.1\n74.6\n\n70.1\n75.3\n\n69.0\n73.7\n\nTable 2. Track-level era classification accuracy (%) on the\nBillboard test set at the decade level, for the seed-77 run\nof each architecture. Across all 18 runs, macro accuracy is\n69.0±2.0 and micro accuracy 75.3±1.5.\nvalidation macro accuracy. To mitigate class imbalance,\ntraining batches were constructed by undersampling each\ndecade to match the size of the smallest class (2000s, 1 585\ntracks). This procedure is re-randomized every epoch. Every architecture was trained under three random seeds, giving the 18 runs over which we report variability below.\n4.3 Model Selection Strategy\nA central design requirement is that the era classifier must\nbe trained exclusively on audio from a single chart culture\nso that its temporal representations are derived from that\nculture alone. Large-scale pre-trained music foundation\nmodels such as MERT or Jukebox are trained on internetscale corpora that very likely include Korean popular music; fine-tuning such models would risk cross-cultural data\nleakage, confounding the measurement we aim to perform.\nWe therefore use CNN architectures trained from\nscratch. We evaluated five models commonly used in\nMIR [16]—FCN [17], ShortChunkCNN and its residual\nvariant (ShortChunkCNN_Res) [18], musicnn [19], and\nCRNN [20]—plus a simple baseline of three 2D convolutional layers (kernel size 3), each followed by batch normalization, ReLU, and max pooling. Model sizes range\nfrom approximately 86 k (baseline) to 837 k parameters.\nThe goal is not to maximize era-prediction accuracy per\nse, but to ensure that whatever temporal patterns the models learn are attributable solely to Billboard audio, making\ncross-domain inference interpretable as a culturally uncontaminated lens.\n5. RESULTS\n5.1 In-Domain Performance on Billboard\nAll six architectures achieved reasonable in-domain performance on the Billboard test set (Table 2), with decadelevel macro accuracy ranging from 67.0 % to 71.2 %, well\nabove the 16.7 % chance level for six-class classification.\nThe year-level confusion matrix (Figure 2) further shows\nthat errors are not random: predictions cluster near the\ndiagonal, indicating that the models capture a meaningful temporal gradient. This tendency weakens around the\n2010s, which may reflect the rapid diversification of genres\nand production techniques in recent popular music.\n\nFigure 2. Confusion matrix of year-level predictions on\nthe Billboard test set, pooled over all runs. Even when\nthe exact year is missed, predictions fall near the true era,\nas the diagonal shows. The tendency weakens around the\n2010s, possibly reflecting greater stylistic diversity in recent music.\n1960s 1970s 1980s 1990s 2000s\nMelon tracks (n)\nMedian offset (yr)\n\n166\n351\n602\n773\n879\n−4.7 −4.5 −4.1 −2.4 −2.7\n±SD 0.6\n0.7\n0.8\n0.8\n1.4\n\nTable 3. Median era offset per decade for Melon songs\nunder Billboard-trained models, mean ± SD across the 18\nruns. The same models are unbiased in domain (Figure 3).\n5.2 Billboard-to-Melon Inference: Main Finding\nWhen Billboard-trained models are applied to Melon\nsongs, the prediction error is no longer centered around\nzero (Figure 3), while on held-out Billboard audio the\nsame models stay centered in every decade, with a median in-domain offset of at most 0.2 years. Melon songs\nare consistently inferred as belonging to earlier Billboard\neras.\nTable 3 summarises the median era offset for each\ndecade. The offset holds near −4.7, −4.5, and −4.1 years\nacross the 1960s, 1970s, and 1980s. The 1960s value is\nbounded from below because the model cannot predict eras\nearlier than 1958, and the Melon corpus begins in 1964:\nits earliest songs can be shifted at most six years into the\npast. In the 1990s the offset contracts to −2.4 years, a step\nreproduced in every run, and holds at that level through\nthe 2000s (−2.7, the least stable value in the table and the\ndecade where in-domain errors are most dispersed as well,\nFigure 3). This pattern is consistent with musicological accounts suggesting that Korean popular music exhibited a\ntemporal offset in adopting globally circulating styles in\nearlier decades, and that this offset narrowed sharply after\nthe 1990s transition without closing.\n\n\fFigure 4. KDE of year-level predictions for Melon songs\n(Billboard → Melon), pooled over all runs. Every decade\npeaks left of zero. The 2000s curve is the only bimodal\none, with mass both at the origin and near −4 years: part\nof the decade has synchronized and part has not. Inset: the\nsame densities on the full prediction range.\n1970s mode\n2000s mode\n\n0.07\n\nFCN\n\n0.06\n\nCNN\n\nCRNN\nSCNNR\nSCNN Musicnn\n\n0.05\n\nDensity\n\nFigure 3. Prediction error distribution (quarter-decade\nlevel) for Billboard (in-domain) and Melon (crossdomain), pooled over all runs, with the y-axis shared\nwithin each decade so the two domains are directly comparable. Billboard errors stay centered on zero in every\ndecade while widening toward the 2000s; Melon errors are\ndisplaced toward earlier eras throughout.\n\n0.04\n0.03\n0.02\n0.01\n\nIn the 2000s the predictions also split in two (Figure 4):\nsome songs are dated on-era, others still several years\nearly, and no single number describes both. The split follows genre. Among the tracks dated on-era in at least\n80 % of runs, the recurring names are dance and hip-hop\nacts (BigBang, 2NE1, Epik High), while those dated consistently early are led by ballad and R&B singers (SG\nWannabe, Brown Eyes, Wheesung).\n5.3 Cross-Architecture Consistency\nThe overall trend is not an artifact of a single architecture.\nFigure 5 compares the 1970s and 2000s predictions across\nall six models. Every model back-dates the 1970s, by between 3.7 and 6.5 years. Moving to the 2000s, four of\nthe six shift four to six years toward zero: on most architectures the distance between the two chart cultures has\nlargely closed over these three decades. The other two\ndissent—the baseline CNN barely moves, and Musicnn\nmoves slightly further back—so how much offset survives\ninto the 2000s remains architecture-dependent.\n5.4 Melon-to-Billboard Reverse Inference\nBack-dating might simply be what a Billboard-trained\nmodel does to unfamiliar audio; reversing the direction\ntests that. Trained on Melon and applied to Billboard, the\nmodels place Billboard songs later on the Melon-derived\ntimeline, by amounts shrinking from about ten years in\nthe 1960s to near zero in the 2000s (Figure 6)—the same\n\n0.00\n\n-15\n\n-10\n\n-5\n\n0\n\nPredicted - True(One Year)\n\n5\n\n10\n\n15\n\nFigure 5. Per-architecture shift of the era offset (Billboard\n→ Melon), each model pooled over its seeds. Arrows run\nfrom a model’s 1970s density mode (orange) to its 2000s\nmode (blue).\nnarrowing, recovered by models that never saw Billboard.\nIt carries less weight: the Melon-trained model reaches\n52.4 % macro accuracy on the artist-disjoint Melon test\nsplit, against 76.3 % for the Billboard-trained models over\nthe same five decades, and 9.5 % for the 1960s, whose\ntraining partition holds 34 tracks. We read it as corroborating the direction of the effect rather than its magnitude.\n6. CONTEXTUALIZING THE ERA OFFSET\nTable 4 summarizes the model’s predictions for five representative Korean artists spanning four decades. We interpret these patterns in light of the structural conditions of\ncross-cultural music flow.\n6.1 Structural Delay under Limited Channels\n(1960s–1970s)\nUntil the late 1980s, Korean musicians accessed American popular music mainly through U.S. military base\nperformances and AFKN radio [37, 39]. Choi Hee-jun,\n\n\fsuch as SM Entertainment and YG Entertainment (the latter founded by his former bandmate Yang Hyun-suk) to\nproduce artists oriented toward global trends from inception. BigBang (YG) and Girls’ Generation (SM) are consistent with this reading, with median offsets near zero by\n2007–09. We stress that a single watershed figure cannot\nbe shown to have caused a corpus-level shift; what the\nmeasurements show is that the structural gap contracted\nsharply around this moment.\n\n7. DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS\nFigure 6. KDE of year-level predictions for Billboard\nsongs (Melon → Billboard), pooled over seeds of the\nMelon-trained model. Billboard songs are predicted toward later eras on the Melon-derived timeline.\nArtist\n\nPeriod\n\nn Median (yr)\n\nSpread\n\nChoi Hee-jun\nSanullim\nSeo Taiji\nBigBang\nGirls’ Gen.\n\n1964–69\n1978–88\n1992–03\n2007–09\n2007–09\n\n12\n13\n10\n14\n6\n\n−6.1±0.7\n−5.2±2.0\n+2.8±2.2\n−0.1±0.8\n−1.0±1.1\n\n−8 to −2\n−13 to +1\n−5 to +10\n−5 to +1\n−6 to +1\n\nTable 4. Year-level era offsets for five representative Korean artists. Median is the mean ± SD of the per-artist\nmedian across runs; spread is the 10th–90th percentile of\nthat artist’s per-song offsets, after excluding two Sanullim\nentries whose audio turned out to be non-original recordings.\nwho has recalled formative listening to artists such as Nat\nKing Cole via military FM [41], and Sanullim, whose\npsychedelic rock drew on styles available through the same\nchannels [38], are back-dated by 6.1 and 5.2 years despite\na full decade separating their activity. That the gap persists\nacross genres and generations is consistent with its being\na property of the distribution infrastructure rather than of\nindividual artists.\n6.2 The Seo Taiji Watershed and Its Aftermath\nSeo Taiji and Boys (debut 1992) are widely credited with\ndismantling the broadcast-network-controlled Korean music market and catalyzing the rise of the talent-agency system that defines contemporary K-pop [34–36]. Shim [34]\nnotes that “each of their albums was in itself a musical experimentation,” spanning new-jack-swing, metal with traditional instruments, gangsta rap, and techno. Seo Taiji is\nthe only artist in Table 4 the model places ahead of his\nchart dates, by +2.8 years and in 15 of 18 runs, at a time\nwhen his decade as a whole is still back-dated by 2.4 years.\nHis songs also spread over a 15-year band, the widest in the\ntable, against six to seven years for the consistently placed\nartists on either side of the transition.\nThis is the transition our decade-level results locate\nin the 1990s, visible in a single career: by breaking the\nbroadcast monopoly, he helped open the way for agencies\n\nWe proposed an era-classification framework to quantify\ncross-cultural temporal alignment. Our main finding—a\nfour-to-five year past-biased era offset in Korean chart music from the 1960s through the 1980s, halving at the 1990s\nand holding at two to three years through the 2000s—gives\nquantitative form to musicological accounts of how globally circulating styles were locally adopted and progressively synchronized.\nThis study has several limitations. Our models cannot\nseparate compositional style from recording technology: a\nmel-spectrogram CNN responds to tape noise, compression, and mastering as readily as to harmony or instrumentation, so part of the measured offset is likely production\nlag rather than stylistic lag. Related factors point the same\nway: 30-second crops give no access to song-level form,\nand artist-aware splitting does not control for producers,\nengineers, or songwriters. Individual songs are dated imprecisely: across the 30-second crops of one song, predicted years vary with a mean SD of 3.3 years on Billboard\nand 6.0 on Melon, so while our decade medians average\nthat noise out, no one song’s offset is a precise dating. The\naudio is matched from YouTube by title and artist and is\nnot always the original recording. Finally, chart data reflects circulation rather than production, and the corpora\ncover different spans.\nTwo of these we can bound. Mastering style alone does\nnot drive the era estimate: across 243 pairs of original and\nremastered audio for the same songs, the predictions that\nchange divide almost evenly between earlier and later, as\nthe mono 16 kHz input intends. And restricting the corpus\nto its best-matched 80 % moves no decade median by more\nthan half a year.\nWe read the era offset not as simple imitation, but as a\ntrace of the evolving structural conditions of cross-cultural\nmusic flows. Future work will extend the framework to\nother chart systems, identify the acoustic cues behind the\noffset, and apply era inference within a single corpus to\nsurface artists predicted into later eras than their own [40].\nOne such case already sits in our Billboard test set: the\nmodels date The Beatles’ 1960s entries about two years\nlater than their chart dates, against essentially zero for the\nother 1960s songs in the split, a gap that holds in 15 of\nthe 18 runs. The Beatles are widely credited with pioneering studio production, and the models place their music\naccordingly.\n\n\f8. ACKNOWLEDGMENT\nThis work was supported by the Ministry of Education of\nthe Republic of Korea and the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF-2024S1A5C3A03046168).\n9. REFERENCES\n[1] M. Regev, Pop-rock music: Aesthetic cosmopolitanism\nin late modernity. Cambridge, UK: Polity, 2013.\n[2] M. Interiano, K. Kazemi, L. Wang, J. Yang, Z. Yu,\nand N. Komarova, “Musical trends and predictability\nof success in contemporary songs in and out of the\ntop charts,” Royal Society Open Science, vol. 5, no. 5,\np. 171274, 2018.\n[3] K. Nie, “Inaccurate prediction or genre evolution? Rethinking genre classification,” in Proc. of the 23rd Int.\nSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Bengaluru, India, 2022, pp. 329–336.\n[4] S. Miroudot, “What’s behind the ‘K’? Common audio\nfeatures of Korean popular music before and after the\nrise of K-POP,” Popular Music, vol. 43, no. 3, pp. 1–28,\n2024.\n[5] M. Verboord and A. Brandellero, “The globalization of\npopular music, 1960–2010: A multi-level analysis of\nmusic flows,” Communication Research, vol. 43, no. 3,\npp. 316–344, 2016.\n[6] J. Serrà, A. Corral, M. Boguñá, M. Haro, and J. L. Arcos, “Measuring the evolution of contemporary western popular music,” Scientific Reports, vol. 2, pp. 1–6,\n2012.\n[7] T. Bertin-Mahieux, D. P. W. Ellis, B. Whitman, and\nP. Lamere, “The million song dataset,” in Proc. of the\n12th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf.,\nMiami, Florida, USA, 2011.\n[8] J. H. Lee, K. Choi, and X. Hu, “K-pop genres: A crosscultural exploration,” in Proc. of the 18th Int. Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Suzhou, China,\n2017, pp. 714–720.\n[9] J. H. Lee and X. Hu, “Cross-cultural similarities and\ndifferences in music mood perception,” in Proc. of the\niConference, 2014, pp. 259–269.\n[10] E. Celen, P. van Rijn, H. Lee, and N. Jacoby, “GlobalMood: A cross-cultural benchmark for music emotion\nrecognition,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.08744, 2025.\n[11] M. Mauch, R. MacCallum, M. Levy, and A. Leroi,\n“The evolution of popular music: USA 1960–2010,”\nRoyal Society Open Science, vol. 2, no. 5, 2015.\n[12] K. Liew, V. Mishra, Y. Zhou, E. V. Epure, R. Hennequin, S. Wakamiya, and E. Aramaki, “Network analyses for cross-cultural music popularity,” in Proc. of\nthe 23rd Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConf., Bengaluru, India, 2022, pp. 298–304.\n\n[13] C. Papaioannou, E. Benetos, and A. Potamianos,\n“From west to east: Who can understand the music of\nthe others better?,” in Proc. of the 24th Int. Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conf., Milan, Italy, 2023.\n[14] X. Hu and J. H. Lee, “A cross-cultural study of music\nmood perception between American and Chinese listeners,” in Proc. of the 13th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Porto, Portugal, 2012, pp. 535–\n540.\n[15] H. Lee, F. Hoeger, M. Schoenwiesner, M. Park, and\nN. Jacoby, “Cross-cultural mood perception in pop\nsongs and its alignment with mood detection algorithms,” in Proc. of the 22nd Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Online, 2021, pp. 1–8.\n[16] M. Won, A. Ferraro, D. Bogdanov, and X. Serra, “Evaluation of CNN-based automatic music tagging models,” in Proc. of 17th Sound and Music Computing,\n2020.\n[17] K. Choi, G. Fazekas, and M. Sandler, “Automatic tagging using deep convolutional neural networks,” in\nProc. of the 17th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., New York, NY, USA, 2016.\n[18] M. Won, S. Chun, O. Nieto, and X. Serra, “Datadriven harmonic filters for audio representation learning,” in ICASSP 2020–2020 IEEE Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing, 2020, pp. 536–540.\n[19] J. Pons, O. Nieto, M. Prockup, E. Schmidt, A. Ehmann,\nand X. Serra, “End-to-end learning for music audio\ntagging at scale,” in Proc. of the 19th Int. Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conf., Paris, France,\n2018.\n[20] K. Choi, G. Fazekas, M. Sandler, and K. Cho, “Convolutional recurrent neural networks for music classification,” in Proc. of 2017 IEEE Int. Conf. on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2017, pp. 1–\n5.\n[21] J. Wehrmann, R. Cerri, and R. Barros, “Hierarchical multi-label classification networks,” in Proc. of the\n35th Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, Stockholm, Sweden, 2018.\n[22] M. J. Jang, “How American popular music influenced\nand propelled Korea’s popular music: Based on popular cultural phenomena,” Ph.D. dissertation, Dept. of\nMusic, Daegu Catholic Univ., 2022.\n[23] J.-H. Kim, “A study on the Korean people’s acceptance\nand enjoyment of American modern popular music,”\nThe Korean Folklore, vol. 56, pp. 67–96, 2012.\n[24] E. Zhang, “The impact of Western popular music in\nKorea: The development of ‘jazz song’ in the first\nhalf of the twentieth century,” Korea Observer, vol. 47,\nno. 3, pp. 651–673, 2016.\n\n\f[25] C.-H. Ham and T.-S. Cho, “A study on the 1970’s\nKorean psychedelic rock (focusing on Shin, Joonghyun),” J. of the Korea Academia-Industrial Cooperation Society, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 694–699, 2016.\n\n[38] P. H. Kim and H. Shin, “The birth of rok: Cultural imperialism, nationalism, and glocalization of rock music\nin South Korea, 1964–1975,” positions: east asia cultures critique, vol. 18, no. 1, pp. 199–230, 2010.\n\n[26] M. J. Jang, “Music study of rock band Sanulrim: Focusing on contemporary Korean songs,” Master’s thesis, Daegu Catholic Univ., 2019.\n\n[39] P. H. Kim, “Korean rock’s journey from group sound to\nindie punk,” in Made in Korea: Studies in Popular Music, H. Shin and S.-A. Lee, Eds. London: Routledge,\n2016, pp. 143–162.\n\n[27] C. Lee, “A study on the change and acceptance of popular music from a social perspective: Focused on the\npop music market since the 20th century,” Master’s thesis, Baekseok Univ., Graduate School of Culture and\nArts, 2020.\n\n[40] N. Collins, “Recording artist career comparison\nthrough audio content analysis,” Royal Society Open\nScience, vol. 12, 2025.\n\n[28] J. Jang, “A study about the sociocultural meaning of\nSeo Tai Ji syndrome,” Master’s thesis, Dong-A Univ.,\n2014.\n[29] S.-W. Lee, “K-Pop: A peculiar encounter of Korean\nand pop music – Inquiry into K-Pop’s Korean authenticity,” J. of Humanities, Seoul National Univ., vol. 73,\nno. 1, pp. 77–103, 2016.\n[30] K. Seong, “Four questions and answers about Seo\nTaiji: Self-reflections,” Sahoebipyung (Social Criticism), vol. 26, pp. 200–211, 2000.\n[31] Prog Rock Journal, “Korean psych rock legends Sanullim reissues of first three albums and a 2LP compilation\nwith later works,” Prog Rock Journal, 2023.\n[32] K. M. Moist, “Introduction – Global psychedelia and\ncounterculture,” Rock Music Studies, vol. 5, no. 3,\npp. 197–204, 2018.\n[33] C. Morrison, “Psychedelic music in San Francisco:\nStyle, context, and evolution,” Ph.D. dissertation, Concordia Univ., 2000.\n[34] D. Shim, “Hybridity and the rise of Korean popular\nculture in Asia,” Media, Culture & Society, vol. 28,\nno. 1, pp. 25–44, 2006.\n[35] E.-Y. Jung, “Articulating Korean youth culture through\nglobal popular music styles: Seo Taiji’s use of rap\nand metal,” in Korean Pop Music: Riding the Wave,\nK. Howard, Ed. Folkestone, UK: Global Oriental,\n2006, pp. 109–122.\n[36] R. Maliangkay, “The popularity of individualism: The\nSeo Taiji phenomenon in the 1990s,” in The Korean\nPopular Culture Reader, K. H. Kim and Y. Choe, Eds.\nDurham, NC: Duke University Press, 2013, pp. 296–\n313.\n[37] R. Maliangkay, “Supporting our boys: American military entertainment and Korean pop music in the 1950s\nand early 1960s,” in Korean Pop Music: Riding the\nWave, K. Howard, Ed. Folkestone, UK: Global Oriental, 2006, pp. 21–33.\n\n[41] H.-J. Choi, “Singer Choi Hee-jun: From law\nschool to the stage” (in Korean), Catholic\nTimes, Nov. 5, 2006. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://www.catholictimes.org/\narticle/200611120153178\n\n\f", "numLines": 807, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 807}}}
